    "md5": "8898d89dbb83d1d0e189499013fd5f54",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1761,
    "chunk_text": "we have to use it to the maximum. To maximize our output, we must also use technology. That is the second area and we are bringing in the University of Colombo\u2019s Institute for Agro-technology and Rural Sciences, which will update the research institutions and work to back up the modernization scheme in Sri Lanka. We are combining the private sector and the public sector together. In most areas, whether it be tea, paddy or other crops, our productivity is low. So, we have to increase productivity. Then, there are problems, as you know, with regard to rubber. There are many issues that we have to resolve in the rubber sector.\n\nThen, we must also apply technologies to industry for our manufacturing. The best example is 3D printing. The 3D printing is going to come into effect. So, we have to be ahead in that. There are so many other areas of technology that we have to get into. We must, somehow or other, get into those. In the next five or six years, we have to establish ourselves in that sphere and in the next decade, say, by 2035, we should be known as a leading country in technology. Our survival depends on it. In the olden days, we had achieved that position. We were the top country in engineering; no one could beat us on engineering. Our structures were, sometimes, better than those of the Egyptians who built pyramids.\n\nWe did not build pyramids; we built semi-circular dagobas, which were far more difficult to build. We had built forts like Sigiriya, which has an amazing hydraulic system. So, look at the combination we had in engineering, that no other country could show any constructions with all that. The only other country which had those skills had been China; China and us. We are a small country, but we have the potential."
  },
