    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1709800251014318.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2024-01-09-2024-01-09-0247",
    "chunk_index": 247,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "8a9a71df2385322d06597935c8a509c1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1599,
    "chunk_text": "But, Sir, that is not the only reason for our decision. What is the context in which this Bill is being brought? There have been talks. The Hon. President announced to the whole world that he is going to find a negotiated solution to the Tamil national question. He first said, before the Independence Day; soon after he became\n\nThis law, Sir, makes the President the appointing authority of the members of the Office for National Unity and Reconciliation on the advice of the Minister. We know what the Minister\u2019s views are; he has already said that this morning. So, what is the point in having this Office? There is absolutely nothing that is going to come about it; this Office has nothing do to with reconciliation. It is essentially an Office that is being created, where you could have the Government decide unilaterally on what they think is reconciliation and then, take it to the grassroots and force it down the throats of a reluctant population. That is what this is all about.\n\nI am also in the Sectoral Oversight Committee which considered this Bill. I did not agree to this Bill. However, there was an Amendment suggested by the Chairman of the Committee, who spoke just before me, where he said, \u201cAt least let the appointing authority be the Constitutional Council; let there be some semblance of independence.\u201d But, that has been rejected by the Government.\n\nSo, this has nothing to do with reconciliation, nothing to do with actually discussing with aggrieved parties like the Tamil and Muslim parties and genuinely trying to\n\n121 122\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0daf\u0dda\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dd9"
  },
